class Scheduler
{
    public function scheduleWork(int $cpuCores, array $files, int $jobSize, int $maximumNumberOfProcesses, int $minimumNumberOfJobsPerProcess): Schedule
    {
        $totalJobs = count($files);
        $numberOfProcesses = min($maximumNumberOfProcesses, ceil($totalJobs / $minimumNumberOfJobsPerProcess));
        $jobsPerProcess = max(1, ceil($totalJobs / $numberOfProcesses));

        $scheduledJobs = array_chunk($files, $jobsPerProcess);

        $schedule = new Schedule();
        foreach ($scheduledJobs as $jobs) {
            $schedule->addProcess(new Process($jobs));
        }

        return $schedule;
    }
}

class Schedule
{
    private array $processes = [];

    public function addProcess(Process $process): void
    {
        $this->processes[] = $process;
    }

    public function getNumberOfProcesses(): int
    {
        return count($this->processes);
    }

    public function getJobs(): array
    {
        return array_map(static function (Process $process) {
            return $process->getJobs();
        }, $this->processes);
    }
}

class Process
{
    private array $jobs;

    public function __construct(array $jobs)
    {
        $this->jobs = $jobs;
    }

    public function getJobs(): array
    {
        return $this->jobs;
    }
}
```

In the `scheduleWork` method, the total number of processes is calculated based on the maximum allowed processes and the minimum jobs per process. The jobs are then evenly distributed among the processes, and a `Schedule` object is created to store the scheduled jobs. The `Schedule` class maintains the processes and provides methods to retrieve the number of processes and their respective jobs.

